-- title:
--   Monodromy equivariance of the level-N dictionary
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \geq 1$ and $j_0 \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, write $H = \mathrm{HahnSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the Hahn series field with rational exponents over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and assume the three level-$N$ hypotheses `FullKernelIsRootAt N`, `FullKernelInjAt N`, `FullKernelDiscAt N`: respectively, that for an elliptic $W/H$ and a point $Q$ of additive order $N$ with $(W.\mathrm{fullKernelQuotient}\,Q\,N).\Delta \neq 0$ the $j$-invariant of that quotient is a root of $\Phi$ specialised at $W.j$, for every datum; that when $W.j$ is transcendental over $\mathbb{Q}$ two such points $Q,Q'$ with equal quotient $j$-invariants generate the same subgroup $\mathbb{Z}Q = \mathbb{Z}Q'$; and that over any algebraically closed field of characteristic $\neq 2$ the quotient by the group generated by a point of order $N$ has nonzero discriminant. The assertion is: for every modular polynomial datum `data` of level $N$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(N)$ vanishing on the $q$-expansion pair), every element $m$ of the monodromy subgroup (the image under `hahnTwistHom` of the characters $\chi : \mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{\times}$ with $\chi(1) = 1$, acting on Hahn series by scaling the coefficient of exponent $a$ by $\chi(a)$), and all $r, r'$ roots in $H$ of $\Phi$ specialised at the $j$-invariant of the near curve $\mathrm{ofJ}(j_0 + t)$ with $r' = m(r)$, the pushforward of subgroups along `nearTransport j₀ m` sends the underlying cyclic subgroup of order $N$ that `dictN` attaches to $r$ to the one it attaches to $r'$.
--
--   This is the monodromy equivariance of the root/cyclic-subgroup dictionary at level $N$: the bijection between roots of the modular polynomial at the near curve and order-$N$ cyclic subgroups of its points intertwines the Galois-type action of the monodromy group on roots with transport of subgroups along the induced automorphism of the near model. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_elliptic_cycSub_orbitMap`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_elliptic_cycSub_orbitMap), where equivariance turns the bijection of sets into a map of monodromy orbits for the subsequent counting argument.
